    null/undefined rather than storing the string [38;5;153m"null"[39m.
  - [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m — returns the stored id, or [38;5;153mnull[39m if absent. Never throws.
  - [38;5;153mclearUserId()[39m — removes the key. Not called anywhere yet; it exists so ending attribution is a defined operation
    rather than something a future form improvises.

  It exposes exactly those three on one global ([38;5;153mwindow.UserTracking[39m) and keeps the key name private. A header comment
  states the trust rule: this value is for log attribution only and must never gate access or be sent as proof of
  identity.

  [1mData flow.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m gains a [38;5;153muserId[39m in its return value — [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m — not a parameter. Since the
  function is still a stub with no network call, it fabricates the id behind a clearly-marked placeholder comment naming
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m as where the real value will come from. The submit handler in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m calls
  [38;5;153mUserTracking.setUserId(result.userId)[39m after a successful login. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets a [38;5;153m<script src="user-tracking.js">[39m
  before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, since the global must exist first. A future form repeats only that script tag and calls [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m.

  [1mError handling.[0m Two real failure modes. [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m can throw — disabled cookies, Safari private mode, quota — so
  both accessors wrap access in try/catch and degrade to "no id" rather than breaking the login flow; tracking must
  never be able to fail a login. And "no id yet" is a normal state, not an error: before login and after a failed login,
  [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m, and consumers log the event with the id field omitted rather than substituting a
  placeholder, so absent attribution is visibly absent in the data instead of silently wrong.

  [1mTesting.[0m Per your tooling answer, no automated tests. Verification is manual in the browser: submit the form, confirm
  the id appears in [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m and in the logged result; reload and confirm it survives; open a new tab and confirm
  it starts clean.

  [1mNot in scope[0m, stated so it doesn't drift in later: no logout, no auth or gating, no real network call, no change to
  [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m, and nothing touching the CommonJS code in [38;5;153msrc/[39m.

  One assumption worth naming: I'm treating "other forms" as additional pages in this same static site loading their own
  script tags. If any of them will instead be a different app or a different origin, [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m won't reach them
  and this design needs revisiting.

  Does this look right? If so I'll write it up as a spec for you to review before any code gets written.

